    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Caucasian Albania, known in Armenian as Aghvank and later in Arabic as Arran, lay in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> between the Greater Caucasus range, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and the Kura river, roughly in the north and centre of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Its name, which reaches us through Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> writers, has no connection with Albania in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a>. The geographer Strabo, writing around the turn of the era, described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albanians">Albanians</a> as a union of 26 tribes, each with its own language, who had once had separate kings but were now ruled by one. A single kingship seems to have formed by the first century BCE, perhaps under the pressure of the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, Pontus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a> that reached the Caucasus in those decades. The first king known by name is Oroeses, who in 66–65 BCE attacked the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a> during his Caucasian campaign and was defeated. The early royal centre was Kabala, near modern Qabala, on the southern slopes of the Greater Caucasus. Many of the kingdom's peoples spoke languages of the north-east Caucasian family, among them the ancestors of the modern Udi, whose language descends from Caucasian Albanian. From the fourth century, according to Armenian and Albanian sources, a branch of the Arsacid family, related to the royal houses of Armenia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, ruled the country.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Albania was a land of farmers and herders. The plains of the Kura and its tributaries supported grain, vines and orchards, irrigated in places, while upland pastures fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and horses; Strabo admired the fertility of the soil, claiming that it yielded crops with little labour. Towns were few and small, among them Kabala and later Partav, the later Barda, on a tributary of the Kura, which grew into the main city of the region. Trade passed along the Caspian coast between the steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> passes, though Albania was never a major commercial power. Society was dominated by great noble families, who held land, followers and fortresses, above a population of free peasants, herders and dependants. The traditional religion included the worship of the sun and, above all, the moon, whose temple Strabo describes with its sacred slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> reached the country from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, and according to tradition King Urnayr was baptised in the fourth century in connection with the mission of Gregory the Illuminator. A church organisation developed with its own catholicos, closely tied to the Armenian Church, and in the early fifth century an alphabet was created for the Albanian language, which Armenian tradition credits to Mesrop Mashtots. Texts in that script were recovered in the 1990s in palimpsest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> at Saint Catherine's Monastery on Mount Sinai.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#marcus-licinius-crassus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus.jpg" alt="Marcus Licinius Crassus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_a_Roman,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sergey Sosnovskiy, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Albanian armies were drawn from the noble houses and their mounted retainers, with infantry levies from the countryside; Strabo claimed that they could field 60,000 foot and 22,000 horse, a figure that should be treated with caution. In 66–65 BCE Oroeses attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a>'s winter quarters and was then defeated in a battle near the Abas river, after which he made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>. In 36 BCE Publius Canidius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, campaigned against the Iberians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albanians">Albanians</a>. Roman soldiers reached the Caspian under the emperor Domitian, when a detachment of the Twelfth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#legion">Legion</a>, Legio XII Fulminata, left a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> inscription at Gobustan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baku">Baku</a>, the easternmost Latin inscription known. The greater danger came from the north: the Alans, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huns">Huns</a> and other steppe peoples, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> by the mountain passes and by the narrow Caspian coastal route at Derbent, and in 135 an Alan invasion struck Albania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#median-empire">Media</a>. Defending these routes became a shared concern of Albania, Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> Albanian cavalry served in Persian campaigns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> records Albanians among the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amida">Amida</a> in 359. The Sasanians fortified the coastal passage at Derbent, and the great walls there, completed largely in the sixth century, long guarded the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shapur-i.jpg" alt="Shapur I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shapour_I_statue.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown after Rawlinson, George, 1812-1902, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Albania was a monarchy whose kings ruled through and alongside the great noble families. Little is known of its institutions before the fourth century, and much of what we know comes from Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> writers and from the <em>History of the Country of Albania</em> attributed to Movses Kaghankatvatsi, compiled in Armenian, probably in the tenth century, from older material. The kings were usually clients of a stronger power: of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a>'s campaign, and from the third century of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a> listed Albania among the lands of his realm in his great inscription on the Ka'ba-ye Zartosht at Naqsh-e Rustam. The Arsacid kings of the fourth and fifth centuries sought to balance Persian overlordship with loyalty to the Christian church. After the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a> between Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> around 387, Albania received the former Armenian provinces of Artsakh and Utik south of the Kura, which greatly enlarged it. The church, led by its own catholicos, became a central institution of the kingdom; the canons issued by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vachagan-iii">Vachagan III</a> the Pious at the council of Aghuen around 488 regulated church discipline, marriage and the relations between clergy and nobility. The royal seat moved from Kabala to Partav during the fifth century, a change that tradition links with the Sasanian king Peroz I.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Caucasian Albania was a frontier society between the Roman and Iranian worlds, between the steppe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a>, and between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Its kingdom shaped the political geography of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> for six centuries, and its church, alphabet and literature made it one of the three Christian cultures of the Caucasus alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a>, although it was the least enduring of them. The Albanian alphabet, attested in the Sinai palimpsests and a handful of inscriptions, has allowed scholars to read the language and to confirm its close relationship with Udi, still spoken by a small community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, notably in the village of Nij. The Albanian church survived the end of the kingship and flourished under the Mihranid princes in the seventh century, when the history of Movses Kaghankatvatsi records the building of churches and a mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huns">Huns</a> of the north Caucasus. After the Arab conquest the church drew ever closer to the Armenian Church, and the Christians of Artsakh and Utik became part of the Armenian world, while the plains were gradually Islamised and later Turkicised. The heritage of Caucasian Albania has become a subject of strong modern interest, and of national controversy, among historians in Azerbaijan and Armenia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire.jpg" alt="Sasanian Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sasanian_Empire_at_its_apex_under_Khosrow_II.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original file by Ro4444, edited by me, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the Albanian kingdom came through the tightening of Sasanian control rather than through a single conquest. In 450–451 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albanians">Albanians</a> joined the Armenian and Iberian rising against the attempt of the Sasanian king Yazdegerd II to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> on the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a>, a rising crushed at the Battle of Avarayr in 451. A further revolt by King Vache II around 459 ended with his abdication, and for about a quarter of a century the kingship lapsed while Persian officials governed the country. The Sasanian king Balash restored it around 485 in the person of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vachagan-iii">Vachagan III</a>, a member of the old royal family, as part of a wider settlement with the Christian Caucasus after renewed Armenian resistance. Vachagan's reign was remembered as a golden age of church building and reform. After his death, around 510, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> abolished the kingship again and governed Albania through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marzban">marzbans</a>, Persian governors based at Partav. Local rule revived in the early seventh century under the Mihranid family, whose princes navigated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabs">Arabs</a>. The Arab conquest from the 640s brought Arran, as it was now called, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a>, and the Albanian language and separate church faded over the following centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Caucasian Albania was the easternmost of the three ancient Christian kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a>, a client state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> that guarded the Caspian passes, created its own alphabet and church, and left a disputed heritage to the modern peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>.</em></p>

